[BEFORE]
A write-back cache only writes a line to memory on eviction.
Each cache line is 64 bytes on most x86 machines.
So the first question is how we find a line in the cache.
[TARGET #5]
We split the address into a tag, an index and an offset.
[AFTER]
The index selects a set, and the tag tells us whether we have a hit.
With a direct mapped cache, every set has exactly one line.
That gives us conflict misses when two addresses map to the same set.